== Adversary classes ==

The threat model distinguishes four '''adversary classes''' (see [[Adversary_Classes]] for the full taxonomy): '''state actor''', '''criminal organisation''', '''lone operator''', and '''inadvertent / environmental'''. Each class is characterized by a distinct combination of capability, motive, and observable signature differential, and each carries a different prior probability for any given operator. Attribution requires explicit handling of these priors; the [[Detection_False_Positive_Budget|FP budget]] uses them quantitatively to tune per-class alarm thresholds.

* '''State actor''' — broad capability, strategic motive, multi-vector sustained operations.
* '''Criminal organization''' — moderate capability, tactical motive, higher tempo / lower sophistication.
* '''Lone operator''' — limited capability, personal motive, short single-vector windows.
* '''Inadvertent / environmental''' — not an adversary; incidental EM emissions affecting all operators in geographic vicinity uniformly.

== Kill-chain progression ==
The threat model adopts a
'''kill-chain analogy''' from cybersecurity practice
(Lockheed-Martin Cyber Kill Chain). Adversarial psionic operations
are modelled as a five-stage progression:

# '''Reconnaissance''' — adversary maps operator locations, schedules, and (for [[Resonance Hijacking]]) Stabilizer emission spectra.
# '''Access''' — adversary establishes spectral or physical-proximity access sufficient to deploy a chosen threat class.
# '''Effect''' — adversary deploys the threat-class payload ([[Photic_Band_Entrainment]], [[Demoralisation Field]], etc.) to produce the intended operator-state perturbation.
# '''Exfil''' — for intelligence-collection operations, adversary removes information acquired via the operator-state perturbation (the operator's response under entrainment is itself the data).
# '''Cleanup''' — adversary terminates the operation in a manner that leaves minimal forensic trace (gradual signal taper rather than sharp termination; correlation with operator schedule rather than absolute time).

Defenses targeting earlier kill-chain stages (recon and access) are generally more cost-effective than defenses targeting later stages.

''See [[Defense in Depth]] for the per-stage device mapping.''

== Per-vector deep-dives ==
Each row of the threat-classes
table above is now anchored by a per-vector deep-dive:

* [[Photic Band Entrainment]] — 3-8 Hz pulsed RF / visible-light flicker exploit; EEG photic-driving response is the operative mechanism.
* [[Cardiac Rate Modulation Attack]] — 0.8-2 Hz envelope on RF carrier; hypothesized vagal-coupling channel.
* [[Demoralisation Field]] — sustained wideband ELF/RF noise; cumulative sympathetic-dominance signature.
* [[Resonance Hijacking]] — replay-attack on operator [[Psi Stabilizer]] emission spectrum.
* [[Psi Direct Threat]] — hypothesized non-EM ψ-field perturbation defined by absence of EM signature; CONTESTED.

== Defense in depth ==
A defensively-postured operator runs overlapping coverage from multiple device-layers; no single device is treated as sufficient. 

''See [[Defense in Depth]] for the full device-vs-threat matrix.'' 

Minimum stack:

* '''[[Psi Ward]]''' at workspace (physical shielding).
* '''[[Psi Scanner]]''' continuous logging (detection).
* '''[[Neural Firewall]]''' when leaving the warded workspace (operator-worn protection).
* Operator-state monitoring (HRV, EEG) for [[Psi_Direct_Threat|ψ-direct]] residual detection.

The framework rejects '''detection without defense''' (knowing you are being attacked but doing nothing) and '''defense without detection''' (defending against unknown threats with no feedback).

For active operations the stack is extended with [[Psi Defender]] (frequency-hopping Stabilizer; signal authentication; multi-antenna direction-finding).

== Detection FP budget ==
Detection thresholds for each [[Psionic Threat Model|threat class]] are tuned against an explicit '''false-positive budget''' rather than against nominal per-event
FP-rates. See [[Detection_False_Positive_Budget]] for the full
calculation. The budget formula:

<math>E[\text{FP per month}]_c = r_c \cdot N \cdot T_c</math>

where '''r_c''' is per-class per-event FP-rate, '''N''' is cohort size, and '''T_c''' is monitoring time-window. The budget scales linearly with cohort size; per-class thresholds must therefore tighten as cohorts grow, because the alarm-fatigue ceiling (~10 false-alarms / investigator / week from NIST 800-94) is per-investigator, not per-operator. Demoralisation-field is the highest-FP class and the [[Psi_Direct_Threat|ψ-direct]] class carries the tightest budget (≤ 1 FP/month) given its very-low evidence-quality.

